WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 92: Thomas Edison and the Menlo Park Invention Factory: The Wizard of Light

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:16.400
[Rebecca] Today, we travel to the rural farmland of Middlesex County, New Jersey, where in the late 1870s, the modern world was quite literally invented.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:29.760
[Rebecca] Before 19-year-old Thomas Alva Edison set up shop along the Pennsylvania Railroad in the hamlet of Menlo Park, scientific invention was largely the solitary pursuit of eccentric tinkerers working in garrets.

5
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.800
[Rebecca] Edison transformed it into a collaborative, systematic industrial enterprise.

6
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.800
[Rebecca] The world's first industrial research and development laboratory.

7
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:48.400
[Rebecca] In just six astonishing years, Edison's muckers captured the human voice on foil and conquered the darkness with the practical incandescent light bulb.

8
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Wizard of Menlo Park.

9
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:28.080
[George] He purchased a modest plot of land in the quiet rural hamlet of Menlo Park, New Jersey, situated 25 miles southwest of New York City along the main line of the Pennsylvania Railroad.

10
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:39.840
[George] There, amidst rolling dairy farms and dirt roads, he constructed a two story white clapboard building that would become the world's first dedicated industrial research and development laboratory.

11
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:47.520
[George] Edison's bold vision was not merely to invent individual machines, but to invent an entirely new method of inventing.

12
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:56.480
[George] He proclaimed with characteristic bravado that his new facility would produce a minor invention every 10 days and a big thing every six months or so.

13
00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:18.040
[George] Unlike traditional inventors who labored alone in secretive basements with limited tools, Edison assembled under one roof a comprehensive arsenal of scientific resources, precision machine tools, chemical retorts, galvanometer testing stations, and a library containing thousands of scientific treatises and patent records from around the globe.

14
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:29.000
[George] Most importantly, Edison assembled an extraordinary interdisciplinary team of brilliant craftsmen, machinists and scientists whom he affectionately dubbed his muckers.

15
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:40.120
[George] The team included Master Swiss machinist John Cruzy, mathematical physicist Francis Upton, glassblower Ludwig Bohm, and chemical experimenter Charles Batchelor.

16
00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:52.200
[George] Together, they operated not as a rigid hierarchy, but as an energetic, around-the-clock guild of creative problem solvers driven by Edison's boundless curiosity and relentless work ethic.

17
00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:04.440
[George] In the summer of 1877, while working on an apparatus to record and repeat telephone messages by embossing indentations onto paraffin paper, Edison made an astonishing intuitive leap.

18
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:17.880
[George] He realized that if the mechanical vibrations of sound waves could indent a moving surface, that same grooved surface could subsequently vibrate a responsive diaphragm and recreate the original acoustic pressure waves in the air.

19
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:35.800
[George] In early December 1877, Edison sketched a rough diagram of a grooved brass cylinder mounted on a threaded steel spindle, flanked by two diaphragms with steel styluses, and handed the sketch to his master machinist, John Cruzi, scrawling a simple directive.

20
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:36.920
[George] Make this.

21
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:43.320
[George] Crusy constructed the device in thirty hours, bringing it to Edison's workbench with skeptical amusement.

22
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:51.520
[George] Edison wrapped a sheet of thin tin foil tightly around the grooved cylinder, adjusted the recording needle, and turned the hand crank.

23
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.560
[George] Leaning his mouth close to the diaphragm, he shouted the simple nursery rhyme.

24
00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:03.600
[George] Mary had a little lamb, its fleece was white as snow, and everywhere that Mary went, the lamb was sure to go.

25
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:11.520
[George] He then reset the cylinder, positioned the playback stylus into the newly indented grooves, and turned the handle once more.

26
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:21.920
[George] To the absolute bewilderment and terror of everyone in the room, the crude tinfoil machine spoke back in Edison's exact voice, thin and metallic yet unmistakable.

27
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.360
[George] Crusey turned pale and crossed himself in shock.

28
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:36.720
[George] No human being in history had ever heard a machine reproduce the human voice, when Edison carried the strange contraption into the offices of Scientific American in New York the following morning.

29
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:46.880
[George] The editors were so stunned by the speaking machine that crowds packed the editorial rooms, and the national press christened the young inventor, the wizard of Menlo Park.

30
00:04:46.880 --> 00:05:00.240
[George] Emboldened by his international fame with the phonograph, Edison turned his sights in 1878 to the supreme technological challenge of the 19th century, the development of a practical incandescent electric light.

31
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:14.400
[George] While arc lamps had already been invented by scientists like Humphrey Davy and Charles Brush, they produced an intensely blinding, hissing glare accompanied by toxic fumes, suitable only for outdoor lighthouses and city squares.

32
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:27.680
[George] The world's interior illumination remained hostage to dim, flickering gas jets and kerosene lamps, which posed constant fire hazards, produced suffocating heat, and stained wallpaper with greasy soot.

33
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:39.320
[George] Dozens of brilliant European and American inventors had attempted to create an incandescent bulb by passing an electric current through a thin filament to make it glow whitehot, but all had failed.

34
00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:52.680
[George] Filaments made of platinum, iridium, or carbon burned to ash in the air within seconds, melted under high temperatures, or required immense amounts of copper wiring that made commercial deployment economically impossible.

35
00:05:52.680 --> 00:06:04.520
[George] Leading British physicists publicly declared that the subdivision of the electric light into small, safe domestic units was a mathematical impossibility that violated the laws of thermodynamics.

36
00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:14.920
[George] Edison, however, approached the problem not merely as the design of a single bulb, but as a complete integrated system, inspired by the municipal gas industry.

37
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:28.600
[George] Edison envisioned a central generating station that would distribute electrical power through underground conduits to thousands of independent homes, allowing each resident to turn individual lamps on and off without affecting their neighbors.

38
00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:47.080
[George] To make this vision economically viable, Edison's mathematical prodigy Francis Upton calculated that the light bulbs must possess high electrical resistance, operating at around 100 volts with minimal current, dramatically reducing the diameter and cost of the copper distribution cables.

39
00:06:47.080 --> 00:07:03.000
[George] To achieve a high-resistance lamp that would glow for hundreds of hours without burning up, Edison realized he needed two critical breakthroughs: an ultra-high vacuum inside the glass bulb and an extraordinary filament material that could withstand extreme thermal stress.

40
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:18.800
[George] Glassblower Ludwig Boehm spent months perfecting delicate springal mercury air pumps, laboriously extracting air until the pressure inside the bulbs was reduced to less than one millionth of an atmosphere, virtually eliminating oxygen molecules that caused oxidation.

41
00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.320
[George] Then began one of the most grueling trial and error scientific quests in human history.

42
00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:31.520
[George] In the laboratory at Menlo Park, Edison and his muckers tested over 6,000 different carbonaceous materials.

43
00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:48.480
[George] They carbonized horsehair, cardboard, cedar shavings, fishing line, coconut hair, spider silk, and even hairs plucked from the beards of laboratory assistants, baking each delicate fiber in crucibles and mounting them between platinum lead wires with steady hands.

44
00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.080
[George] On the evening of October 21, 1879, the breakthrough arrived.

45
00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:06.400
[George] Edison's team carbonized a simple length of ordinary cotton sewing thread, carefully shaped it into a delicate hairpin horseshoe, sealed it inside a blown glass globe, and exhausted the atmosphere.

46
00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:13.920
[George] When electric current was applied, the filament began to glow with a soft, steady, beautiful lemon-colored radiance.

47
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:19.680
[George] The muckers gathered around the workbench, expecting the fragile thread to snap within minutes.

48
00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:30.800
[George] Instead, the lamp burned hour after hour through the night and into the following day, ultimately illuminating the room for forty continuous hours before the filament finally burned through.

49
00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.360
[George] The practical electric light was born.

50
00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:42.240
[George] To convert the world to electric illumination, Edison knew he needed a public spectacle so magnificent that no skeptic could doubt its reality.

51
00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:56.000
[George] He declared that on New Year's Eve, 1879, Menlo Park would be lit up by the power of electricity, inviting the general public, Wall Street financiers, and city officials to witness the dawn of a new age.

52
00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:07.160
[George] The Pennsylvania Railroad ran special excursion trains from Philadelphia and Jersey City, carrying over 3,000 awestruck visitors into the dark winter countryside of New Jersey.

53
00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:14.200
[George] As the trains pulled into Menlo Park station, passengers stepped out into an otherworldly fairy tale.

54
00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:25.880
[George] Edison had strung insulated copper wires from poles across the hamlet, illuminating the laboratory, office buildings, and muddy country lanes with dozens of glowing incandescent bulbs.

55
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:36.280
[George] Inside the laboratory, glass globes glowed silently from chandeliers, reflecting off brass machinery and polished wood without flame, smoke or odor.

56
00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:46.280
[George] Spectators gasped in wonder, reaching out with trembling fingers to touch the cool glass, unable to comprehend how a brilliant light could burn without burning out.

57
00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:53.480
[George] A few weeks later, Edison discovered an even superior filament material, carbonized Japanese bamboo.

58
00:09:53.480 --> 00:10:06.600
[George] A strip of bamboo fiber, split microscopically by hand, proved capable of burning for over 1,200 continuous hours, becoming the commercial standard that powered Edison lamps for the next two decades.

59
00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:18.680
[George] The New Year's Eve demonstration permanently shattered public skepticism, sending gas company stocks tumbling on Wall Street and unleashing an avalanche of investment capital to electrify America.

60
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:28.040
[George] Having conquered the laboratory, Edison now had to prove that his electric system could operate at full industrial scale in the heart of the world's commercial capital.

61
00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:39.000
[George] In 1881, he packed up his Menlo Park operations and moved to Lower Manhattan, establishing the Edison Electric Illuminating Company at 257 Pearl Street.

62
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:56.960
[George] There, inside an old brick commercial building, Edison supervised the installation of four massive 30-ton steam-driven dynamos affectionately named Jumbos, while his crews ripped up the streets of Manhattan to lay 15 miles of subterranean copper conduits encased in heavy iron pipes.

63
00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:06.080
[George] On the afternoon of September 4, 1882, at precisely 3 in the afternoon, Edison donned a frock coat and stepped into the library of J.

64
00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.680
[George] Pierpont Morgan at the corner of Broad and Wall Streets.

65
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.320
[George] At a signal from Edison, the master switch was thrown at Pearl Street.

66
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:28.720
[George] Instantly, 400 incandescent bulbs flared to life across a one square mile district of Lower Manhattan, illuminating Morgan's banking house, the editorial offices of the New York Times, and hundreds of financial offices.

67
00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:36.160
[George] For the first time in human history, electric power was central, metered, and accessible at the turn of a switch.

68
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:47.760
[George] Typists worked under clear, cool light without the oppressive headaches caused by gas combustion, and newspaper editors laid out late editions without fear of open flames igniting newsprint.

69
00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:59.440
[George] Pearl Street was the architectural prototype for the modern electrical grid, sparking a worldwide infrastructure revolution that would light London, Paris, Tokyo, and beyond within a decade.

70
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:12.880
[George] Thomas Edison went on to hold 1,093 United States patents, a staggering record of productivity that included the motion picture camera, alkaline storage batteries, and commercial electric trains.

71
00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:20.640
[George] Yet of all his monumental contributions to civilization, none eclipsed the foundational legacy of Menlo Park itself.

72
00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:33.800
[George] In that modest New Jersey workshop, Edison did not just invent products, he invented the institutional architecture of modern innovation, team-based, data-driven, interdisciplinary industrial research.

73
00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:48.760
[George] Every modern corporate research institution, from Bell Laboratories and General Electric to IBM, NASA, and Silicon Valley Tech campuses traces its organizational DNA directly to Edison's Menlo Park Laboratory.

74
00:12:48.760 --> 00:13:06.120
[George] He proved that technological progress need not rely on rare, isolated strokes of genius, but can be systematically pursued through collective experimentation, rigorous methodology, and what he famously described as 1% inspiration and 99% perspiration.

75
00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:21.400
[George] Today, a towering 131-foot Art Deco Memorial Tower, crowned by a giant 13-foot glass light bulb, stands on the site of the original laboratory in Menlo Park, now renamed the Township of Edison, New Jersey.

76
00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:30.200
[George] The original wooden laboratory buildings were purchased and lovingly relocated to Greenfield Village in Michigan by his lifelong friend Henry Ford.

77
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:35.240
[George] But the spirit of Menlo Park lives everywhere that electric light dispels the night.

78
00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:44.120
[George] Every time a recorded voice speaks from a speaker, and in every laboratory where human curiosity dares to illuminate the future.

